variables multiplied together at
a time.

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:15.920
OK,

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:18.060
and quad means two.

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:20.240
Even though it sounds like four,
two squared is four, which is

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:21.820
why it's quadratic.

00:14:21.820 --> 00:14:22.380
OK,

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:24.480
so this is like how you would
make the equivalent of a

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:28.230
parabola, but when you have
multiple input variables.

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:28.780
All right,

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:31.920
this reduces to a parabola if x
is just a scalar, right,

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:33.420
ax squared.

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:36.070
So all right.

00:14:36.100 --> 00:14:40.600
What was the gradient of the
quadratic form?

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:45.320
What was the gradient here?

00:14:45.320 --> 00:14:50.520
It was a plus a transpose
multiplied by the vector x.

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:51.760
Awesome.

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:55.760
The gradient of x transpose ax
is a plus a transpose x.

00:14:55.760 --> 00:14:57.700
We're not going to walk through
the derivation of this one.

00:14:57.700 --> 00:14:59.420
It's linked in the notes if you
would like.

00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:01.080
The first two you should be
familiar with.

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:02.980
We talked about it on Tuesday,

00:15:02.980 --> 00:15:06.340
and discussed it deeply in the
notes.

00:15:06.340 --> 00:15:09.940
There is a simplification you
can make to the quadratic form

00:15:09.940 --> 00:15:13.690
one if A is symmetric.

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:19.410
Can you remind me what it means
for a matrix to be symmetric?

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:21.560
What is a symmetric matrix?

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:23.120
A is the same as a transpose.

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:25.040
Yeah, A and A transpose are the
same.

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:29.040
And what does this gradient
reduce to in that case, where A

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:32.240
and A transpose are the same.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:34.400
It's not a trick.

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:35.900
What is the reduced to? Yeah.

00:15:35.900 --> 00:15:36.640
2AX.

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:38.140
Awesome, 2AX.

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:42.260
Also, that kind of makes sense,
because here,

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:44.920
if A were the identity matrix,

00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:47.880
X transpose X would be the same
as the squared length of X.

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:50.780
So plugging in the identity
matrix here should give us the

00:15:50.780 --> 00:15:54.530
same result as the second
gradient rule.

00:15:54.820 --> 00:15:56.780
So these are our big three
gradient rules.

00:15:56.780 --> 00:16:02.580
And all of the functions defined
in terms of matrix

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:05.600
vector operations that we're
going to need to work with for

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:08.140
the rest of today's lecture

00:16:08.140 --> 00:16:11.240
will use one of these three
rules, or one or more of these

00:16:11.240 --> 00:16:12.280
three rules.

00:16:12.280 --> 00:16:14.360
But just with these three rules,

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:17.200
we can find relatively
complicated

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:21.440
gradients.

00:16:21.700 --> 00:16:24.040
And so that's what I want us to
work on for a little bit.

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:25.580
So the first example

00:16:25.580 --> 00:16:31.320
is the gradient

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:37.120
of the norm.

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:38.820
Not the squared norm,

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:40.320
the norm.

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:42.220
OK.

00:16:42.220 --> 00:16:45.780
So you worked on this in lab
yesterday.

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:48.080
And what I want to get at is
that there are two ways of

00:16:48.080 --> 00:16:50.330
thinking about this.

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:59.440
Or I'll call it two approaches.

00:17:01.080 --> 00:17:05.300
The first approach is to just
pretend we didn't know about any

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:07.100
of the big three rules

00:17:07.100 --> 00:17:10.980
and expand this out as a
function of all of its input

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:11.980
variables.

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:18.220
So suppose x is made up of the
components x1,

00:17:18.220 --> 00:17:20.470
x2, through xd,

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:25.200
f of x then,

00:17:25.820 --> 00:17:31.070
how would I write it out in
terms of its components?

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:35.180
Square root of x1 squared plus
x2 squared plus dot dot.

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:39.400
The square root of x1 squared
plus x2 squared plus all the way

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:41.650
through xd squared.

00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:44.100
Now, if I wanted to find its
gradient,

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:46.660
I could forget about these big
three rules.

00:17:46.660 --> 00:17:50.340
And I could just go to what?
Like, well, what could I always

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:51.340
use?

00:17:56.940 --> 00:18:01.800
What could I do here to find the
gradient of that?

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:02.440
Yeah.

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:03.940
The derivative.

00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:04.680
Close.

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:07.440
Find the partial derivatives
with respect to each one of the

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:08.660
input variables.

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:09.660
Good.

00:18:10.280 --> 00:18:13.280
So I could find.

00:18:13.800 --> 00:18:16.400
Now, the nice thing with this
one is that all of the input

00:18:16.400 --> 00:18:19.980
variables appear symmetrically
in the formula.

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:23.380
What that means is if I shuffled
around the x1s, x2s, x3s, the

00:18:23.380 --> 00:18:24.940
formula would look the same.

00:18:24.940 --> 00:18:27.740
So I really only need to
consider the partial derivative

00:18:27.740 --> 00:18:30.480
with respect to one of them, and
all of the other ones are going

00:18:30.480 --> 00:18:33.860
to be the same just with the
subscript changed.

00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:39.110
So why don't I just start with
x1?

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:43.480
What is the partial derivative
of this beast with respect to

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:44.480
x1?

00:18:53.920 --> 00:18:58.740
Yeah.
Is it 2x1 over the square root

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:01.220
of all that again? Right.
And so what you're using here is

00:19:01.220 --> 00:19:04.120
the chain rule, which is that if
all we had was the square root

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:05.420
of x,

00:19:05.420 --> 00:19:09.520
the derivative would be 1 over 2
times the square root of x.

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:12.400
Or another way to think about it
is that if you had x to the 1

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:17.500
half, the derivative is 1 by 2x
to the negative 1 by 2,

00:19:17.500 --> 00:19:19.860
which is 1 over 2 times the
square root of x.

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:22.860
But we don't just have the
square root of x here because

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:25.320
there's not just one x.
There are dx's.

00:19:25.320 --> 00:19:26.820
So the chain rule says,

00:19:26.820 --> 00:19:29.070
take the derivative

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:32.400
of the square root operation,

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:36.900
but keep whatever is under the
square root.

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:44.200
And then multiply that by the
derivative of what's in the

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:46.680
square root with respect to x1,

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:49.680
which is indeed 2x1.

00:19:50.360 --> 00:19:54.760
But then notice the 2 up top and
the 2 down here cancel.

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:58.980
And so we're left with just x1
over the square root of x1

00:19:58.980 --> 00:20:00.960
squared plus x2 squared plus x3
squared.

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:05.460
And what is that? Well, that's
just the length of x.

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:08.720
OK.

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:10.220
So similarly,

00:20:13.420 --> 00:20:15.720
the partial with respect to x2,

00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:20.220
what would that end up being?

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:26.370
Yeah? The x2 over norm x? Yeah,
x2 over the length of x.

00:20:29.540 --> 00:20:31.180
OK.
Let me save some space here.

00:20:38.640 --> 00:20:41.980
And so all of them, the other
ones would be found similarly.

00:20:41.980 --> 00:20:45.840
So if I stitch these all
together in the gradient,

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:50.160
what this tells me is the
gradient

00:20:50.160 --> 00:20:53.620
contains x1 over the length of
x,

00:20:53.620 --> 00:20:57.020
x2 over the length of x,

00:20:57.020 --> 00:21:02.270
all the way through xd over the
length of x.

00:21:04.080 --> 00:21:07.680
But can you think of another way
to write this,

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:09.180
perhaps more

00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:11.640
concisely?

00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:16.600
Just tell me the gradient of f
with respect to x has the

00:21:16.600 --> 00:21:19.380
components x1 over box,

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:23.240
x2 over box, x3 over box, all
the way through xd over box.

00:21:23.240 --> 00:21:24.540
Turns out that box is a
constant.

00:21:24.540 --> 00:21:25.060
So yeah?

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:30.340
What can we do, Sam? Yes, you
can just do x over the

00:21:30.340 --> 00:21:33.340
vector x over that.
Exactly.

00:21:33.860 --> 00:21:36.780
These components are just the
components of the vector x.

00:21:36.780 --> 00:21:42.040
So we can write this as the
vector x

00:21:42.040 --> 00:21:45.140
over this one.

00:21:47.020 --> 00:21:49.920
So there's a nice interpretation
to this.

00:21:49.920 --> 00:21:55.100
The gradient of the norm is a
unit vector in the direction of

00:21:55.100 --> 00:21:58.100
x, okay? Because remember,

00:21:58.460 --> 00:22:02.200
x over this length of x is a
unit vector that points in the

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:05.760
same direction as x, but has a
length of 1,

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:06.580
okay?

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:12.400
So this is how I might find a
gradient of something I

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:14.940
didn't tell you about.

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:19.260
But you might say, well hold up,
we didn't actually use any of

00:22:19.260 --> 00:22:21.540
the three rules to do this.

00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:24.680
Could we have perhaps leveraged
our prior work a little bit

00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:25.520
more?

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:26.860
There is a way to do it,

00:22:26.860 --> 00:22:30.420
and it involves the chain rule
for gradients.

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:33.420
Let's talk about that.

00:22:36.040 --> 00:22:41.200
I want us to think of this
function

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:47.120
as performing two successive
operations to an input vector x.

00:22:49.760 --> 00:22:52.010
The first operation,

00:22:54.040 --> 00:22:56.540
and sort of what I'm getting at
is I want to break it down into

00:22:56.540 --> 00:22:57.840
two operations,

00:22:57.840 --> 00:23:01.640
both of which are simpler to
think about than the overall

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:03.740
action of taking the norm.

00:23:03.740 --> 00:23:07.640
And by simpler, I mean uses
things we've seen already.

00:23:07.640 --> 00:23:09.040
Here's what I'm getting at.

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:14.000
I'm going to apply one function
to get something, and then

00:23:14.000 --> 00:23:19.160
another function to get
something else.

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:22.960
And I'll make this blue.

00:23:22.960 --> 00:23:28.440
And what comes out at the end is
the length of x.

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:30.660
What do you think this
intermediate step I'm alluding

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:32.160
to is?

00:23:37.780 --> 00:23:39.260
Maybe to hint,

00:23:39.260 --> 00:23:40.760
I'll say,

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:42.940
ideally,

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:49.160
we use the fact that,

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:53.120
which of the big three gradient
rules seems most relevant to

00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:54.620
this situation?

00:23:57.140 --> 00:23:59.260
The squared norm.

00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:03.620
The squared norm.
the squared norm is 2 times the

00:24:03.620 --> 00:24:05.120
vector x.

00:24:08.420 --> 00:24:13.340
So how might I do that here?

00:24:13.340 --> 00:24:17.500
What I'm saying is that this
first step could involve just

00:24:17.500 --> 00:24:22.660
taking the squared norm of x,
and the second step can involve

00:24:22.660 --> 00:24:25.960
taking the square root of
whatever came before it.

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:29.800
So I'm thinking of two separate
functions here.

00:24:29.800 --> 00:24:31.320
This function h,

00:24:31.320 --> 00:24:37.080
which is scalar to scalar, and
this

00:24:37.080 --> 00:24:37.920
function,

00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:39.320
let's call it g,

00:24:39.320 --> 00:24:43.070
which is vector to scalar.

00:24:44.480 --> 00:24:47.480
So here's my proposal.

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:54.300
Define the function g of x to
just do the square norm of x.

00:24:56.740 --> 00:24:59.940
Then define the function h.
I could say it takes in the

00:24:59.940 --> 00:25:01.860
input x, but that makes it a
little confusing.

00:25:01.860 --> 00:25:05.080
Why don't we just say h of y or
something?

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:08.600
All it does is take the square
root of y.

00:25:08.600 --> 00:25:11.080
These are fundamentally
different types of functions.

00:25:11.340 --> 00:25:14.840
g takes in a vector and returns
a number.

00:25:14.840 --> 00:25:18.020
h takes in a number and returns
a number.

00:25:18.020 --> 00:25:21.620
And the reason this function, h,
takes in a number is because the

00:25:21.620 --> 00:25:24.180
output of the first thing was
already number right the squared

00:25:24.180 --> 00:25:27.020
length of a vector is a number
so if I want to do things out on

00:25:27.020 --> 00:25:32.920
to that I need to use a function
that only takes in a number

00:25:32.920 --> 00:25:36.640
okay and what I'm saying is that
my overall function F

00:25:36.640 --> 00:25:40.980
can really be thought of

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:46.760
as calling this second operation
the square root machine H

00:25:46.760 --> 00:25:49.760
on Then the input,

00:25:51.020 --> 00:25:52.020
g,

00:25:52.920 --> 00:25:55.420
of the vector to scalar
function,

00:25:55.420 --> 00:25:56.420
x.

00:25:57.000 --> 00:25:59.680
And so another way to think
about what I'm saying

00:25:59.680 --> 00:26:04.720
is that g takes in a vector, x,
and returns a scalar.

00:26:04.720 --> 00:26:07.640
That scalar it returns is the
squared length of x.

00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:11.240
Then this h function takes the
square root of whatever was past

00:26:11.240 --> 00:26:12.240
it.

00:26:12.530 --> 00:26:14.030
All right.

00:26:15.720 --> 00:26:21.180
And so the chain rule

00:26:21.180 --> 00:26:26.240
here

00:26:26.240 --> 00:26:31.040
says the following.

00:26:31.840 --> 00:26:37.480
The gradient of f is equal to
the

00:26:37.480 --> 00:26:38.480
derivative.

00:26:38.680 --> 00:26:40.080
Notice I'm not saying partial
derivative.

00:26:40.080 --> 00:26:41.380
I'm just saying derivative,

00:26:41.380 --> 00:26:45.760
because h is just a regular
scalar to scalar function, like

00:26:45.760 --> 00:26:48.080
the type you knew about before
this class.

00:26:48.080 --> 00:26:53.760
The derivative of h when you
pass in g of

00:26:53.760 --> 00:26:56.380
x times,

00:26:56.880 --> 00:27:02.060
what do you think we follow this
up with?

00:27:02.060 --> 00:27:04.940
If g was a scalar to scalar
function,

00:27:04.940 --> 00:27:07.260
you'd follow this up with g
prime of x.

00:27:07.260 --> 00:27:08.940
That's what the regular chain
would tell you.

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:11.520
But g is not a scalar to scalar
function.

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:12.960
g doesn't have a derivative.

00:27:12.960 --> 00:27:15.960
It has a gradient.

00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:22.100
So I follow this up with the
gradient of g.

00:27:22.500 --> 00:27:25.380
So this is sort of the same as
the chain rule you knew about

00:27:25.380 --> 00:27:26.380
before,

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:29.920
just a little more general,
because now it accounts for the

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:32.200
fact that g and h are different
types of functions.

00:27:32.200 --> 00:27:33.820
g takes in a vector,

00:27:33.820 --> 00:27:36.300
and so the way you describe its
rate of change is with the

00:27:36.300 --> 00:27:38.560
gradient.
H takes in a scalar, so the way

00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:41.900
you describe its rate of change
is with a scalar.

00:27:41.900 --> 00:27:45.580
So it's still the derivative of
the outside thing with the

00:27:45.580 --> 00:27:48.920
inside thing passed in times the
rate of change of the inside

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:50.300
thing.
It's just the rate of change of

00:27:50.300 --> 00:27:52.580
the inside thing is now a
gradient rather than a single

00:27:52.580 --> 00:27:53.580
derivative.

00:27:56.900 --> 00:28:02.150
So what would that tell us here?

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:12.050
It tells us the gradient of the
norm of x is equal to what?

00:28:15.440 --> 00:28:19.380
The H here is the square root
machine and it's just so we have

00:28:19.380 --> 00:28:23.130
it written down even though
we've already used it

00:28:24.720 --> 00:28:29.970
What is H prime of Y here

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:33.360
H prime of Y.

00:28:35.500 --> 00:28:36.500
Yeah.

00:28:37.240 --> 00:28:38.800
Good.
1 over 2 root Y.

00:28:39.080 --> 00:28:44.330
So over here, what am I gonna
write?

00:28:45.620 --> 00:28:46.040
Based

00:28:46.040 --> 00:28:56.180
on

00:28:56.180 --> 00:28:59.940
this 2 root g of x

00:28:59.940 --> 00:29:05.700
times the gradient of g of

00:29:05.700 --> 00:29:06.700
x.

00:29:07.340 --> 00:29:08.980
OK, but these are things we've
seen before.

00:29:08.980 --> 00:29:10.860
Because what is g of x here?

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:13.860
It's the squared norm.

00:29:14.080 --> 00:29:16.580
And the gradient of the squared
norm is a solved problem.

00:29:16.580 --> 00:29:19.560
It's one of the three big three
rules.

00:29:19.560 --> 00:29:21.810
And then also,

00:29:22.120 --> 00:29:25.940
what is the square root of the
squared norm?

00:29:25.940 --> 00:29:31.190
That is, what is the square root
of g of x?

00:29:31.660 --> 00:29:35.120
What's the square root of g of
x?

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:35.680
Yeah.

00:29:35.680 --> 00:29:37.140
It's the norm.
It's just the norm.

00:29:37.140 --> 00:29:40.600
The square root of the squared
norm is just the regular norm.

00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:45.100
Multiply that by the gradient of
the squared norm, which is 2x.

00:29:45.120 --> 00:29:50.370
And lo and behold, you get the
same thing we had before.

00:29:50.920 --> 00:29:56.170
But we didn't have to take any
individual partial derivatives.

00:29:57.940 --> 00:30:02.440
And we more easily used things
we already knew about.

00:30:06.180 --> 00:30:12.120
So that's the chain rule for
vector to scalar functions.

00:30:14.940 --> 00:30:18.620
To apply this, I want you to
work on another example from

00:30:18.620 --> 00:30:20.120
yesterday's lab.

00:30:22.660 --> 00:30:28.460
The function f of x, which
returns the dot product of a

00:30:28.460 --> 00:30:30.710
with x squared.

00:30:31.960 --> 00:30:34.700
Once again, there are two ways
to approach this.

00:30:34.700 --> 00:30:39.540
However you want, I want you to
approach it and find the

