00:52:14.140 --> 00:52:16.020
for them that I want.

00:52:16.020 --> 00:52:19.900
And what I will do here is I
will write w transpose x

00:52:19.900 --> 00:52:21.400
transpose y.

00:52:25.720 --> 00:52:27.900
We still haven't taken the
gradient yet.

00:52:27.900 --> 00:52:29.280
But now when we do that,

00:52:29.280 --> 00:52:33.300
it's not going to require much
work.

00:52:33.300 --> 00:52:37.120
So I take the gradient of R.

00:52:37.120 --> 00:52:41.200
Let me just make it clear here
that that's R itself.

00:52:41.200 --> 00:52:43.700
The gradient of R with respect
to w,

00:52:43.700 --> 00:52:46.040
we still have this 1 over n
factor floating around.

00:52:46.040 --> 00:52:47.600
We'll keep it around.

00:52:47.600 --> 00:52:49.360
Now we can just go term by term.

00:52:49.360 --> 00:52:52.200
What's the gradient of this
first thing with respect to w?

00:52:52.200 --> 00:52:52.720
0.

00:52:52.720 --> 00:52:53.660
We can keep moving.

00:52:53.660 --> 00:52:55.860
What's the gradient of this
second term,

00:52:55.860 --> 00:52:58.720
everything that I've circled
here, minus 2w transpose x

00:52:58.720 --> 00:53:02.470
transpose y? What's the gradient
of that with respect to w?

00:53:07.340 --> 00:53:11.840
What is the gradient of this
with respect to w?

00:53:13.720 --> 00:53:15.480
Yeah.
It would be negative 2x

00:53:15.480 --> 00:53:16.660
transpose y.

00:53:16.660 --> 00:53:17.100
Awesome.

00:53:17.100 --> 00:53:22.580
Negative 2x transpose y.

00:53:25.740 --> 00:53:28.980
It looks scary, but remember, x
transpose y, though it involves

00:53:28.980 --> 00:53:30.760
a matrix, it's just a vector.

00:53:30.760 --> 00:53:33.580
Whenever you multiply a matrix
by a vector,

00:53:33.580 --> 00:53:36.880
x transpose by y comes out as a
new vector.

00:53:36.880 --> 00:53:40.440
So this is the dot product of w,
which is a vector with x

00:53:40.440 --> 00:53:42.100
transpose y, which is a vector.

00:53:42.100 --> 00:53:45.850
And that gradient, as we've
seen, is just the other vector.

00:53:49.820 --> 00:53:53.340
Then what is the gradient of the
last thing?

00:53:53.340 --> 00:53:56.520
w transpose x transpose x w.
W.

00:53:56.520 --> 00:54:01.020
What is the gradient of that
with respect to W?

00:54:07.200 --> 00:54:12.840
x transpose x plus transpose of
x transpose x all times

00:54:12.840 --> 00:54:13.660
W.

00:54:13.660 --> 00:54:14.540
Good.
Indeed,

00:54:14.540 --> 00:54:20.060
it would be x transpose x plus x
transpose x transpose, a lot of

00:54:20.060 --> 00:54:21.060
transposes,

00:54:21.500 --> 00:54:25.140
times W.
But what do we know about x

00:54:25.140 --> 00:54:26.640
transpose x?

00:54:26.940 --> 00:54:28.100
Yeah.
It has to be symmetric.

00:54:28.220 --> 00:54:29.720
It's symmetric.
Why?

00:54:30.800 --> 00:54:32.060
It's the same matrix.

00:54:32.060 --> 00:54:33.520
I don't really know how to
describe and test that.

00:54:33.520 --> 00:54:34.780
Well, there's a couple of ways
you can think about it.

00:54:34.780 --> 00:54:38.200
One is if you distributed the
transpose operation here,

00:54:38.200 --> 00:54:42.360
it would be the second thing
transposed times the first thing

00:54:42.360 --> 00:54:44.940
transposed.
But then you'd have a double

00:54:44.940 --> 00:54:47.540
transpose, and that would cancel
out and just give you x.

00:54:47.540 --> 00:54:50.320
But remember, there is also the
convenient interpretation of x

00:54:50.320 --> 00:54:53.380
transpose x, which is that it
contains the dot products of all

00:54:53.380 --> 00:54:56.140
pairs of the columns of x.

00:54:56.140 --> 00:55:00.440
Element at position 7 comma 5 is
the dot product of column 7 with

00:55:00.440 --> 00:55:01.940
column 5.

00:55:01.940 --> 00:55:05.460
And the element at position 5
comma 7 is also the dot product

00:55:05.460 --> 00:55:07.760
with column 5 and column 7.

00:55:07.760 --> 00:55:10.240
So the matrix is symmetric.

00:55:10.240 --> 00:55:15.860
So we have 2 times

00:55:15.860 --> 00:55:19.610
x transpose x times w.

00:55:27.180 --> 00:55:28.180
Okay,

00:55:28.760 --> 00:55:31.940
so let's write this again.

00:55:31.940 --> 00:55:36.960
We found that the gradient of
mean squared error is negative 2

00:55:36.960 --> 00:55:38.600
over n,

00:55:39.120 --> 00:55:40.560
sorry I skipped a step there,

00:55:40.560 --> 00:55:46.020
1 over n times negative 2 x
transpose y plus

00:55:46.020 --> 00:55:49.770
2 x transpose x, w,

00:55:50.820 --> 00:55:54.920
which I can now factor out the
negative 2 here

00:55:54.920 --> 00:55:57.680
and write this as x transpose y

00:55:57.680 --> 00:56:03.600
minus x transpose x,

00:56:03.860 --> 00:56:04.860
w.

00:56:06.380 --> 00:56:09.100
What's our final step here? If
