to x1 and the partial derivative
of x2.

01:05:12.240 --> 01:05:13.760
If you do that,

01:05:13.760 --> 01:05:17.510
the gradient looks like this.

01:05:19.020 --> 01:05:22.060
But it's very hard, if not
impossible, to solve for where

01:05:22.060 --> 01:05:26.560
the gradient is exactly equal to
0.

01:05:27.700 --> 01:05:31.000
This is the setting in which we
actually work in practice.

01:05:31.000 --> 01:05:35.020
We would need to minimize
functions whose gradients exist,

01:05:35.020 --> 01:05:37.920
but we can't solve for where
they're equal to 0.

01:05:37.920 --> 01:05:41.220
And the type of function we will
ultimately really care about,

01:05:41.220 --> 01:05:43.820
like in this most recent example
we worked with,

01:05:43.820 --> 01:05:47.820
is one that describes mean
squared error, or more generally

01:05:47.820 --> 01:05:49.260
empirical risk,

01:05:49.260 --> 01:05:51.700
for some very complicated model.

01:05:51.700 --> 01:05:56.460
The setting you should be
thinking about is these large

01:05:56.460 --> 01:06:00.220
language models with billions or
trillions of parameters where

01:06:00.220 --> 01:06:03.970
their average loss function is
some really complicated thing,

01:06:04.040 --> 01:06:07.040
like more complicated than
squared loss.

01:06:07.380 --> 01:06:11.300
And so finding the gradient of
the loss is possible, or the

01:06:11.300 --> 01:06:14.280
gradient of the empirical risk
is possible, but solving for

01:06:14.280 --> 01:06:17.360
where it is equal to 0 is not
possible.

01:06:17.360 --> 01:06:20.360
And so this hypothetical
function

01:06:20.700 --> 01:06:24.280
is meant to be an estimate of
that kind of setting, where you

01:06:24.280 --> 01:06:25.440
have a function,

01:06:25.440 --> 01:06:28.380
you have its gradient, gradient,
but you don't know where the

01:06:28.380 --> 01:06:31.340
gradient is equal to zero.

01:06:31.340 --> 01:06:35.520
And so the question is, if we
have the gradient,

01:06:35.520 --> 01:06:35.860
well,

01:06:35.860 --> 01:06:38.440
maybe we can't solve for where
the gradient is zero.

01:06:38.440 --> 01:06:41.180
But the gradient still does tell
us something.

01:06:41.180 --> 01:06:43.480
What does the gradient tell us?

01:06:43.480 --> 01:06:45.360
If I'm on a point,

01:06:45.360 --> 01:06:46.620
say,

01:06:46.960 --> 01:06:47.960
here,

01:06:48.220 --> 01:06:52.720
what does the gradient at that
point tell me?

01:06:54.700 --> 01:06:57.100
It has some geometric meaning,
and what was that geometric

01:06:57.100 --> 01:06:58.100
meaning?

01:07:03.680 --> 01:07:05.280
What did the gradient tell me?

01:07:05.280 --> 01:07:06.340
The steepest descent?

01:07:06.340 --> 01:07:07.360
Yeah, it tells me,

01:07:07.360 --> 01:07:10.360
if I have this thing that tells
me the gradient at any point,

01:07:10.360 --> 01:07:13.260
it tells me the direction along
which the function is increasing

01:07:13.260 --> 01:07:14.760
the quickest.

01:07:15.360 --> 01:07:18.380
What I want to think about now
is how could I estimate where

01:07:18.380 --> 01:07:21.000
the minimum of the function is.

01:07:21.000 --> 01:07:24.700
If I have the gradient, it tells
me the direction along which I

01:07:24.700 --> 01:07:28.120
could walk to increase my
function's value as quickly as

01:07:28.120 --> 01:07:28.900
possible.

01:07:28.900 --> 01:07:31.920
So if I'm trying to decrease my
function's value,

01:07:31.920 --> 01:07:34.920
what should I do?

01:07:35.220 --> 01:07:39.720
Yeah, I should move opposite the
direction of the gradient,

01:07:40.020 --> 01:07:41.420
negative the gradient,

01:07:41.420 --> 01:07:45.420
if my goal is to try and get to
the bottom of this hill.

01:07:45.420 --> 01:07:46.280
OK?

01:07:46.280 --> 01:07:47.860
Let me start with a simpler
example.

01:07:47.860 --> 01:07:52.100
that is a scalar-to-scalar
function.

01:07:58.520 --> 01:08:01.260
There's a default example I want
to copy in here.

01:08:01.260 --> 01:08:04.260
Yeah, there we go.

01:08:04.720 --> 01:08:08.300
This is a good example of the
type of function whose

01:08:08.300 --> 01:08:10.660
derivative is really easy to
calculate,

01:08:10.660 --> 01:08:14.320
but solving where the derivative
is 0 is annoying to calculate.

01:08:14.320 --> 01:08:15.060
Right?

01:08:15.060 --> 01:08:19.180
This is a degree 4 polynomial in
just one variable,

01:08:19.180 --> 01:08:20.180
x.

01:08:20.600 --> 01:08:23.020
So its derivative is a degree 3
polynomial.

01:08:23.020 --> 01:08:28.100
There's no quick formula for
finding the roots of a degree 3

01:08:28.100 --> 01:08:29.520
polynomial.
It would be kind of annoying.

01:08:29.620 --> 01:08:31.520
You'd have to try and factor it
and whatnot.

01:08:31.520 --> 01:08:32.520
OK.

01:08:33.420 --> 01:08:36.080
So what does the derivative here
tell you?

01:08:36.080 --> 01:08:41.330
Well, if you plug in a point
like there, All right.

01:08:42.200 --> 01:08:45.950
What does the derivative there
tell you?

01:08:47.220 --> 01:08:49.500
Yeah? The slope of the tangent
line at that point? Yeah.

01:08:49.500 --> 01:08:53.250
And at this particular point,
what would it tell you?

01:08:53.460 --> 01:08:57.340
This is what the slope is? Yeah,
and what is it at that point?

01:08:57.340 --> 01:08:59.540
Not the exact slope, but tell me
what you know about the slope at

01:08:59.540 --> 01:09:01.040
that point.
Negative? Yeah.

01:09:01.280 --> 01:09:05.780
So let me use red here.

01:09:09.680 --> 01:09:13.260
Here, derivative is negative.

01:09:15.600 --> 01:09:18.180
So what does that tell me? What
that tells me,

01:09:18.180 --> 01:09:21.400
again, you have to imagine you
don't have the ability to look

01:09:21.400 --> 01:09:23.180
at the entire function with your
eyes.

01:09:23.180 --> 01:09:26.280
All you have is a formula that
tells you the derivative at any

01:09:26.280 --> 01:09:27.980
point you plug it.

01:09:27.980 --> 01:09:30.280
If you're at a point,

01:09:30.280 --> 01:09:31.820
the derivative tells you,

01:09:31.820 --> 01:09:34.020
this derivative box tells you
the derivative right now is

01:09:34.020 --> 01:09:35.020
negative.

01:09:35.460 --> 01:09:38.720
And your goal is to guess where
the bottom of the function is.

01:09:38.720 --> 01:09:40.760
And again, we're guessing
because we don't have a way of

01:09:40.760 --> 01:09:42.120
figuring out where it is
exactly.

01:09:42.120 --> 01:09:44.060
That's the whole premise here.

01:09:44.060 --> 01:09:47.900
If you know the derivative here
is negative, what would you do?

01:09:47.900 --> 01:09:50.340
What direction would you move
it?

01:09:50.340 --> 01:09:52.590
Left or right?

01:09:56.980 --> 01:09:57.860
Yeah.

01:09:57.860 --> 01:09:59.360
You would move right.
Right,

01:09:59.360 --> 01:10:01.680
this is telling you the
derivative is negative, which

01:10:01.680 --> 01:10:04.700
means if you move to the right,
the function is decreasing.

01:10:04.700 --> 01:10:06.960
And so if your goal is to try
and estimate where the bottom

01:10:06.960 --> 01:10:07.760
is,

01:10:07.760 --> 01:10:10.600
you would move to the right.

01:10:10.600 --> 01:10:11.820
So update guess

01:10:11.820 --> 01:10:17.680
by moving right.

01:10:20.280 --> 01:10:24.420
Then there's a question of how
far right do you move.

01:10:24.420 --> 01:10:27.260
Maybe you move a little bit to
the right.

01:10:27.260 --> 01:10:29.400
The thing is, if you move too
far to the right, well, then you

01:10:29.400 --> 01:10:31.740
might overcorrect and end up
over here.

01:10:31.740 --> 01:10:34.000
So at some level in this
process,

01:10:34.000 --> 01:10:38.500
we're going to have to control
by how much we are moving.

01:10:38.500 --> 01:10:41.860
Suppose I did move some amount
to the right and I ended up over

01:10:41.860 --> 01:10:42.860
here.

01:10:43.860 --> 01:10:47.060
I can think of this procedure as
being an iterative one.

01:10:47.060 --> 01:10:48.620
Now I've ended up here.

01:10:48.620 --> 01:10:52.370
What does the derivative here
tell me?

01:10:54.700 --> 01:10:58.900
So at the second point, what is
the derivative?

01:10:58.900 --> 01:10:59.900
Positive,

01:10:59.920 --> 01:11:02.540
and so where should I move?
Should I continue moving to the

01:11:02.540 --> 01:11:03.540
right?

01:11:04.080 --> 01:11:07.160
No, because if a positive
derivative tells me if I move to

01:11:07.160 --> 01:11:07.820
the right,

01:11:07.820 --> 01:11:09.340
the function is going to go up.

01:11:09.340 --> 01:11:12.200
And what I'm trying to do is I'm
trying to go down,

01:11:12.200 --> 01:11:17.450
so I should move to the left.

01:11:23.560 --> 01:11:24.560
OK,

01:11:27.700 --> 01:11:31.420
and then I can move left a
little bit, and then reassess.

01:11:31.420 --> 01:11:34.880
Where did I end up?

01:11:34.880 --> 01:11:37.560
The sort of mental model you
should have for this is, imagine

01:11:37.560 --> 01:11:42.680
you're on some really tall,
hilly mountain with lots of

01:11:42.680 --> 01:11:43.600
little valleys.

01:11:43.600 --> 01:11:47.100
And your goal is to get to the
bottom of the mountain

01:11:47.100 --> 01:11:48.320
as quickly as you can.

01:11:48.320 --> 01:11:49.520
But you can't see the whole
thing.

01:11:49.520 --> 01:11:51.640
It's really foggy.
So all you can see is like a

01:11:51.640 --> 01:11:54.140
small area around you.

01:11:54.140 --> 01:11:55.960
What would you do?

01:11:55.960 --> 01:11:59.020
You'd look at what's around you,
look at the direction that's

01:11:59.020 --> 01:12:00.240
going downhill,

01:12:00.240 --> 01:12:02.040
walk a little bit in that
direction,

01:12:02.040 --> 01:12:05.000
and then reassess where to go
based on the new information you

01:12:05.000 --> 01:12:06.220
have at that point.

01:12:06.220 --> 01:12:07.780
You look around here.

01:12:07.780 --> 01:12:09.120
That way is going downhill.

01:12:09.120 --> 01:12:10.300
I walk.

01:12:10.300 --> 01:12:12.020
Now I look at where it's going
downhill.

01:12:12.020 --> 01:12:13.200
That way is most downhill.

01:12:13.200 --> 01:12:14.300
I go that way.

01:12:14.300 --> 01:12:17.900
And I keep updating my guess
until hopefully I get to a point

01:12:17.900 --> 01:12:18.740
that's flat,

01:12:18.740 --> 01:12:21.340
at which case Hopefully I'm at
the bottom, but maybe I got

01:12:21.340 --> 01:12:22.840
trapped somewhere.

01:12:23.880 --> 01:12:28.500
That is the general procedure
that we are getting at here.

01:12:29.400 --> 01:12:32.080
And that procedure is called the
gradient descent.

01:12:32.080 --> 01:12:34.200
Why is it called gradient
descent?

01:12:34.200 --> 01:12:36.360
Well, it's because the goal is
to try and minimize the

01:12:36.360 --> 01:12:37.360
function.

01:12:37.520 --> 01:12:39.660
Why is it called gradient
descent?

01:12:39.660 --> 01:12:43.440
Well, because this is a scalar
to scalar function, so we were

01:12:43.440 --> 01:12:45.180
thinking in terms of its
derivative.

01:12:45.180 --> 01:12:47.480
But more generally, we want to
minimize vector to scalar

01:12:47.480 --> 01:12:48.440
function,

01:12:48.440 --> 01:12:52.940
and their rates of change are
the gradient.

01:12:52.980 --> 01:12:53.740
OK?

01:12:53.740 --> 01:12:56.540
The analog of what we just
discussed here

01:12:56.540 --> 01:13:00.100
in this more sophisticated
example would be to start with

01:13:00.100 --> 01:13:01.720
some initial guess,

01:13:01.720 --> 01:13:03.080
like there,

01:13:03.080 --> 01:13:05.260
compute the gradient.
The gradient would tell you the

01:13:05.260 --> 01:13:07.280
direction along which the
function is increasing the

01:13:07.280 --> 01:13:07.780
quickest.

01:13:07.780 --> 01:13:09.940
So you go opposite the gradient
a little bit.

01:13:09.940 --> 01:13:10.960
So from there,

01:13:10.960 --> 01:13:12.460
maybe I go down here.

01:13:12.460 --> 01:13:14.160
And then I reassess.

01:13:14.160 --> 01:13:16.420
The gradient will be pointing
upwards, So I'm going to point

01:13:16.420 --> 01:13:19.020
go downwards here.
The gradient will be pointing

01:13:19.020 --> 01:13:20.020
upwards.
So I go down.

01:13:20.220 --> 01:13:23.840
So I keep doing that until I
converge

01:13:23.840 --> 01:13:27.480
now in this case, I would have
converged in a Trap because my

01:13:27.480 --> 01:13:29.540
goal would be to minimize the
overall function.

01:13:29.540 --> 01:13:32.420
I don't have I would have ended
up here So it also depends on

01:13:32.420 --> 01:13:33.460
where you start.

01:13:33.460 --> 01:13:36.860
There are a lot of parameters to
this Algorithm where you start

01:13:36.860 --> 01:13:39.140
matters how big of a step
matters?

01:13:39.140 --> 01:13:41.020
You take matters.
We're gonna explore all of those

01:13:41.020 --> 01:13:44.400
things in the next seven minutes
and onto in Tuesday's life So

01:13:44.400 --> 01:13:46.120
that's the general idea,

01:13:46.120 --> 01:13:47.120
OK?

01:13:48.680 --> 01:13:51.760
So let me write out the
algorithm, and then we can play

01:13:51.760 --> 01:13:54.200
around with it.
There are a lot of examples

01:13:54.200 --> 01:13:55.200
here.

01:14:14.380 --> 01:14:17.720
The goal is to minimize

01:14:17.720 --> 01:14:23.680
the vector to scalar function

01:14:23.680 --> 01:14:27.540
f given its gradient.

01:14:31.820 --> 01:14:35.540
Again, if we could easily solve
for where its gradient is equal

01:14:35.540 --> 01:14:36.520
to 0,

01:14:36.520 --> 01:14:37.880
you wouldn't need to estimate
it.

01:14:37.880 --> 01:14:40.800
You could just write out a
formula for what the minimizer

01:14:40.800 --> 01:14:41.880
would be.

01:14:41.880 --> 01:14:45.630
That's why when we were
minimizing mean squared error,

01:14:45.640 --> 01:14:47.520
we didn't need this gradient
descent idea.

01:14:47.520 --> 01:14:49.980
We could just figure out where
the bottom was without needing

01:14:49.980 --> 01:14:51.740
to do this guesswork.

01:14:51.740 --> 01:14:54.100
We're thinking about more
complicated functions now where

01:14:54.100 --> 01:14:57.840
the gradient exists, but there's
no closed form solution for the

01:14:57.840 --> 01:14:58.840
minimizer.

01:14:59.940 --> 01:15:04.440
And so I'll just keep this
picture over here to the right.

01:15:07.780 --> 01:15:10.780
Here's how it works.

01:15:12.120 --> 01:15:14.960
First, choose an initial guess

01:15:14.960 --> 01:15:20.300
because this is an iterative
procedure.

01:15:20.820 --> 01:15:23.800
You have to start somewhere for
where we think the minimizer

01:15:23.800 --> 01:15:27.900
might be, and then from there we
update our guesses.

01:15:27.900 --> 01:15:29.860
The notation I will use for that

01:15:29.860 --> 01:15:34.360
is the vector x with the
superscript 0 in parentheses.

01:15:36.020 --> 01:15:40.100
The updated guess will be x1,
with the 1 in superscript,

01:15:40.100 --> 01:15:45.880
then x2, and so on and so forth.

01:15:45.880 --> 01:15:48.600
And there's one more thing we
have to choose.

01:15:48.600 --> 01:15:51.640
I said there are two sort of
knobs we have to tune with this

01:15:51.640 --> 01:15:52.640
operation,

01:15:53.240 --> 01:15:57.740
where we start and then also
something else.

01:16:00.280 --> 01:16:03.180
We have to decide where we
start.

01:16:03.180 --> 01:16:07.680
But we also have to decide
something else.

01:16:09.440 --> 01:16:11.690
There it is.

01:16:12.640 --> 01:16:13.640
Yeah.
Learning rate?

01:16:13.840 --> 01:16:18.380
Yeah, how much we travel in the
direction that we decide to

01:16:18.380 --> 01:16:19.380
travel.

01:16:20.220 --> 01:16:22.020
At this point,

01:16:22.020 --> 01:16:25.360
it tells me the direction along
which the function is increasing

01:16:25.360 --> 01:16:26.860
the quickest.
That would be the gradient.

01:16:27.100 --> 01:16:29.440
So the negative of that is the
direction along which it's

01:16:29.440 --> 01:16:31.460
decreasing the quickest.

01:16:31.460 --> 01:16:34.900
But I still have to pick how far
to travel in that direction,

01:16:34.900 --> 01:16:38.400
right? I could update my guess a
little bit or a lot.

01:16:38.400 --> 01:16:40.120
Again, I don't have eyes in this
setting.

01:16:40.120 --> 01:16:41.840
I don't know what the function
actually looks like.

01:16:41.840 --> 01:16:42.720
So I have to,

01:16:42.720 --> 01:16:45.980
like a thing I will have to tune
is how far to go in the

01:16:45.980 --> 01:16:48.100
direction it tells me to go.

01:16:48.100 --> 01:16:52.180
That is another thing to decide,
and so what that is called is

01:16:52.180 --> 01:16:54.430
the learning rate,

01:16:56.560 --> 01:16:59.200
sometimes also called a step
size,

01:16:59.200 --> 01:17:03.540
and the variable we will use in
this class for that is the

01:17:03.540 --> 01:17:05.040
scalar alpha.

01:17:08.040 --> 01:17:13.780
Then what we are going to do is
we are going to update our

01:17:13.780 --> 01:17:19.620
guess iteratively using

01:17:19.620 --> 01:17:21.120
this formula.

01:17:22.020 --> 01:17:27.580
My guess at time step t plus 1
is equal to what?

01:17:27.580 --> 01:17:29.860
Based on everything we've
explored here,

01:17:29.860 --> 01:17:33.610
what is the gradient descent
update rule?

01:17:35.880 --> 01:17:40.060
How do What do you think we'd
write this out?

01:17:40.060 --> 01:17:41.060
Yeah?

01:17:44.380 --> 01:17:45.880
Very smart.

01:17:46.980 --> 01:17:48.680
Gradient times alpha.

01:17:48.680 --> 01:17:49.680
Good.

01:17:49.860 --> 01:17:50.300
Well,

01:17:50.300 --> 01:17:53.020
so instead of x0 here, I'll
write xt.

01:17:53.020 --> 01:17:56.120
So if you plug in t equals 0,
this tells you how to make t

01:17:56.120 --> 01:17:58.920
equals 1, for example.

01:17:58.920 --> 01:18:04.170
Then I have to take the gradient
of f, oops, not that.

01:18:04.720 --> 01:18:09.220
The gradient of f at the
previous guess

01:18:10.860 --> 01:18:14.600
and multiply it by this scalar
alpha,

01:18:14.600 --> 01:18:18.200
because this is how I'm
controlling

01:18:18.200 --> 01:18:23.450
how far to go in the direction
of the gradient.

01:18:24.040 --> 01:18:28.200
Now the question is, what sign
do I put here?

01:18:28.200 --> 01:18:32.600
Is it a plus or is it a minus?

01:18:32.600 --> 01:18:33.580
Yeah,

01:18:33.580 --> 01:18:34.540
it's a minus.

01:18:34.540 --> 01:18:37.320
Because remember, we want to
update by going opposite the

01:18:37.320 --> 01:18:39.000
direction of the gradient.

01:18:39.000 --> 01:18:41.240
The gradient says go up,

01:18:41.240 --> 01:18:43.740
or the gradient says move to the
right.

01:18:43.740 --> 01:18:46.360
Well, it's telling us how to
increase quickly, but we want to

01:18:46.360 --> 01:18:48.120
decrease.
So we go opposite what it tells

01:18:48.120 --> 01:18:50.370
us to do.

01:18:51.220 --> 01:18:54.660
We move against

01:18:54.660 --> 01:18:57.780
the gradient.

01:19:01.100 --> 01:19:01.900
Now,

01:19:01.900 --> 01:19:04.900
when do we stop?

01:19:10.560 --> 01:19:13.260
We're not just going to run this
for loop forever.

01:19:13.260 --> 01:19:14.720
When do we say, all right,

01:19:14.720 --> 01:19:15.740
we're at the minimum,

01:19:15.740 --> 01:19:20.080
or we're close enough to it?

01:19:20.080 --> 01:19:21.080
Yeah?

01:19:21.120 --> 01:19:22.120
Zero,

01:19:22.260 --> 01:19:22.900
close enough.

01:19:22.900 --> 01:19:25.150
If what's zero?

01:19:25.240 --> 01:19:26.000
Gradient.

